By the frozen verdict wording the first is "more extreme than the
family". The repeat is a different draw of generators and not a
preregistered confirmation; it did not reproduce the effect and had
under-dispersed arm means. This does not explain the first result. The
first study had one flagged generator in 384 (B1); the same family type
here has two in 40. Treat generator-level heterogeneity as unresolved,
with more generator draws as the next measurement.

== Limits
<limits>
- One class at one larger size, one seed set; power about 18 % per
  curve, 2 % class-wide at 100 seeds. A null does not exclude smaller
  effects.
- Same shared VM as the first run (Xeon \@ 2.10 GHz, 4 threads,
  isolation L0). Wall-time columns are unisolated observations, not
  admitted performance evidence.
- The reviewer-noted native KS tie defect and shared-seed covariance
  concerns on the first study apply to this tool too; the Kruskal–Wallis
  and ANOVA values above do not depend on the KS routine.
- No ecbench spec/session/audit records exist for this run (AGENTS.md
  §12); it cannot support a comparison claim.

